<onlyinclude>The '''infosphere''' is an environment of information, informational entities and their interactions. The philosopher Luciano Floridi develops the term to explain how information and communication technologies change human environments and our understanding of ourselves.

== What belongs to it? ==
In a narrower use, the infosphere includes the records, communication systems, people and software involved in producing, exchanging and interpreting information. It is broader than the internet: a printed document or an offline record can be part of an informational environment. Floridi also develops a wider philosophical interpretation in which entities and their relations can be understood informationally.

For example, a hospital appointment can involve a physical patient, a telephone conversation, an electronic record, software and a clinical decision. These are not two completely independent worlds, one digital and one physical.

== Inforgs ==
Floridi calls connected informational organisms '''inforgs'''. This is a philosophical description of human identity in an increasingly information-mediated environment, not a biological species or a claim that humans must be physically augmented with electronics. A software agent may share the environment with people without necessarily being alive, conscious or morally equivalent to a person.

== Why it matters ==
The framework asks what happens when personal identity, decisions, privacy and institutions increasingly depend on information. It motivates questions about who can collect and change information, which entities are affected and what harms or responsibilities arise.

The broad view that reality can be understood informationally is a philosophical position. It does not follow from the mathematical theory of communication and is not an experimentally proven theory that everything is made of digital data.

